 "captions": [
  {
   "text": "Ever wondered why your ears pop on a plane?",
   "start": 1,
   "end": 81
  },
  {
   "text": "Here's what's actually happening inside your head.",
   "start": 89,
   "end": 183
  },
  {
   "text": "Behind your eardrum sits a tiny pocket of air - your middle ear.",
   "start": 192,
   "end": 325
  },
  {
   "text": "On the ground, the pressure inside matches the air outside. Perfectly balanced.",
   "start": 333,
   "end": 483
  },
  {
   "text": "But as the plane climbs, the cabin pressure drops.",
   "start": 492,
   "end": 581
  },
  {
   "text": "The trapped air pushes outward, and your eardrum bulges, like a balloon.",
   "start": 590,
   "end": 731
  },
  {
   "text": "That's the muffled, blocked feeling.",
   "start": 739,
   "end": 805
  },
  {
   "text": "Then a tiny tube to your throat - the Eustachian tube - cracks open.",
   "start": 813,
   "end": 953
  },
  {
   "text": "Air escapes, the pressure evens out, and... POP!",
   "start": 962,
   "end": 1067
  },
  {
   "text": "Landing is actually worse.",
   "start": 1076,
   "end": 1125
  },
  {
   "text": "The tube doesn't open on its own as easily, so your eardrum gets pushed inward.",
   "start": 1134,
   "end": 1289
  },
  {
   "text": "That's why swallowing, yawning, or chewing gum helps - they pull the tube open.",
   "start": 1297,
   "end": 1456
  },
  {
   "text": "Follow for more weird things your body does.",
   "start": 1464,
   "end": 1540
  }
 ],
